#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:189
msgid "libuv work queue"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:191
msgid ""
"``uv_queue_work()`` is a convenience function that allows an application "
"to run a task in a separate thread, and have a callback that is triggered"
" when the task is done. A seemingly simple function, what makes "
"``uv_queue_work()`` tempting is that it allows potentially any third-"
"party libraries to be used with the event-loop paradigm. When you use "
"event loops, it is *imperative to make sure that no function which runs "
"periodically in the loop thread blocks when performing I/O or is a "
"serious CPU hog*, because this means that the loop slows down and events "
"are not being handled at full capacity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:200
msgid ""
"However, a lot of existing code out there features blocking functions "
"(for example a routine which performs I/O under the hood) to be used with"
" threads if you want responsiveness (the classic 'one thread per client' "
"server model), and getting them to play with an event loop library "
"generally involves rolling your own system of running the task in a "
"separate thread.  libuv just provides a convenient abstraction for this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:207
msgid ""
"Here is a simple example inspired by `node.js is cancer`_. We are going "
"to calculate fibonacci numbers, sleeping a bit along the way, but run it "
"in a separate thread so that the blocking and CPU bound task does not "
"prevent the event loop from performing other activities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:212
msgid "queue-work/main.c - lazy fibonacci"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:218
msgid ""
"The actual task function is simple, nothing to show that it is going to "
"be run in a separate thread. The ``uv_work_t`` structure is the clue. You"
" can pass arbitrary data through it using the ``void* data`` field and "
"use it to communicate to and from the thread. But be sure you are using "
"proper locks if you are changing things while both threads may be "
"running."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:224
msgid "The trigger is ``uv_queue_work``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:226
msgid "queue-work/main.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:233
msgid ""
"The thread function will be launched in a separate thread, passed the "
"``uv_work_t`` structure and once the function returns, the *after* "
"function will be called on the thread the event loop is running in. It "
"will be passed the same structure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:238
msgid ""
"For writing wrappers to blocking libraries, a common :ref:`pattern "
"<baton>` is to use a baton to exchange data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:241
msgid ""
"Since libuv version `0.9.4` an additional function, ``uv_cancel()``, is "
"available. This allows you to cancel tasks on the libuv work queue. Only "
"tasks that *are yet to be started* can be cancelled. If a task has "
"*already started executing, or it has finished executing*, "
"``uv_cancel()`` **will fail**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:246
msgid ""
"``uv_cancel()`` is useful to cleanup pending tasks if the user requests "
"termination. For example, a music player may queue up multiple "
"directories to be scanned for audio files. If the user terminates the "
"program, it should quit quickly and not wait until all pending requests "
"are run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:251
msgid ""
"Let's modify the fibonacci example to demonstrate ``uv_cancel()``. We "
"first set up a signal handler for termination."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:254 ../../src/guide/threads.rst:264
#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:274
msgid "queue-cancel/main.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:260
msgid ""
"When the user triggers the signal by pressing ``Ctrl+C`` we send "
"``uv_cancel()`` to all the workers. ``uv_cancel()`` will return ``0`` for"
" those tasks that are pending execution and could be cancelled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:271
msgid ""
"For tasks that do get cancelled successfully, the *after* function is "
"called with ``status`` set to ``UV_ECANCELED``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:281
msgid ""
"``uv_cancel()`` can also be used with ``uv_fs_t`` and "
"``uv_getaddrinfo_t`` requests. For the filesystem family of functions, "
"``uv_fs_t.errorno`` will be set to ``UV_ECANCELED``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:287
msgid ""
"A well designed program would have a way to terminate long running "
"workers that have already started executing. Such a worker could "
"periodically check for a variable that only the main process sets to "
"signal termination."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:294
msgid "Inter-thread communication"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:296
msgid ""
"Sometimes you want various threads to actually send each other messages "
"*while* they are running. For example you might be running some long "
"duration task in a separate thread (perhaps using ``uv_queue_work``) but "
"want to notify progress to the main thread. This is a simple example of "
"having a download manager informing the user of the status of running "
"downloads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:302 ../../src/guide/threads.rst:328
#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:339 ../../src/guide/threads.rst:349
msgid "progress/main.c"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:309
msgid ""
"The async thread communication works *on loops* so although any thread "
"can be the message sender, only threads with libuv loops can be receivers"
" (or rather the loop is the receiver). libuv will invoke the callback "
"(``print_progress``) with the async watcher whenever it receives a "
"message."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:316
msgid ""
"It is important to realize that since the message send is *async*, the "
"callback may be invoked immediately after ``uv_async_send`` is called in "
"another thread, or it may be invoked after some time. libuv may also "
"combine multiple calls to ``uv_async_send`` and invoke your callback only"
" once. The only guarantee that libuv makes is -- The callback function is"
" called *at least once* after the call to ``uv_async_send``. If you have "
"no pending calls to ``uv_async_send``, the callback won't be called. If "
"you make two or more calls, and libuv hasn't had a chance to run the "
"callback yet, it *may* invoke your callback *only once* for the multiple "
"invocations of ``uv_async_send``. Your callback will never be called "
"twice for just one event."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:335
msgid ""
"In the download function, we modify the progress indicator and queue the "
"message for delivery with ``uv_async_send``. Remember: ``uv_async_send`` "
"is also non-blocking and will return immediately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:345
msgid ""
"The callback is a standard libuv pattern, extracting the data from the "
"watcher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:347
msgid "Finally it is important to remember to clean up the watcher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:356
msgid ""
"After this example, which showed the abuse of the ``data`` field, "
"bnoordhuis_ pointed out that using the ``data`` field is not thread safe,"
" and ``uv_async_send()`` is actually only meant to wake up the event "
"loop. Use a mutex or rwlock to ensure accesses are performed in the right"
" order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:363
msgid ""
"mutexes and rwlocks **DO NOT** work inside a signal handler, whereas "
"``uv_async_send`` does."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:366
msgid ""
"One use case where ``uv_async_send`` is required is when interoperating "
"with libraries that require thread affinity for their functionality. For "
"example in node.js, a v8 engine instance, contexts and its objects are "
"bound to the thread that the v8 instance was started in. Interacting with"
" v8 data structures from another thread can lead to undefined results. "
"Now consider some node.js module which binds a third party library. It "
"may go something like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:373
msgid ""
"In node, the third party library is set up with a JavaScript callback to "
"be invoked for more information::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:385
msgid ""
"``lib.do`` is supposed to be non-blocking but the third party lib is "
"blocking, so the binding uses ``uv_queue_work``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:388
msgid ""
"The actual work being done in a separate thread wants to invoke the "
"progress callback, but cannot directly call into v8 to interact with "
"JavaScript. So it uses ``uv_async_send``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../src/guide/threads.rst:392
msgid ""
"The async callback, invoked in the main loop thread, which is the v8 "
"thread, then interacts with v8 to invoke the JavaScript callback."
msgstr ""

